@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` already has a private `parsePointer` (RFC 6901 parsing and validation) and `resolvePointer`. RFC 6902 operations act on the parent of the target location, so the patch code needs pointer parsing but not `resolvePointer` itself.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** all six operations per RFC 6902, no input mutation, errors on any invalid patch or failed operation.
+**Non-Goals:** JSON Merge Patch, patch generation (diff), in-place application, unrelated validation of the document beyond JSON values.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Reuse `parsePointer`** from the same module for `path` and `from`.
+- **Copy-on-start:** deep-clone the document once (`structuredClone`) and mutate the clone; `value` members from the patch are deep-cloned when inserted so the result shares nothing with the patch. A failure throws, discarding the clone, so no partial result escapes.
+- **Shared primitives:** `getParent(doc, tokens)` locating the container, plus `addAt`, `removeAt`, `replaceAt` used by the operations. `move` = remove + add; `copy` = clone + add; `replace` = check existence + set.
+- **Array indices** use the same strict rule as the pointer module (`0` or no leading zeros); `-` is allowed only for add targets.
+- **Prototype safety:** object members are set with `Object.defineProperty` (so `__proto__` becomes an own member) and existence checked with `Object.hasOwn`.
+- **Test equality:** a small recursive `deepEqual` implementing the RFC 6902 rules (order-insensitive objects, ordered arrays, numbers by value).
+- **Whole-document paths:** an empty path for add/replace returns the new value as the document; remove of the root is an error (no valid result in JSON terms is defined) — assumption recorded here.
+- **Move prefix rule:** compare token arrays; `from` a proper prefix of `path` throws; identical paths are a no-op after checking existence.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- Cloning costs O(document size) per call; acceptable for the stated use, and required for non-mutation.
+- Errors are plain `Error`s, consistent with `resolvePointer`.
